WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.251 --> 00:00:06.253
It's dark days for democracy.

00:00:06.878 --> 00:00:09.256
And our crisis is global.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.884
If you're like me,
you can feel it in your bones.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.638
My expertise focuses on democracy
in the United States,

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:17.847
where I live and work.

00:00:17.847 --> 00:00:21.851
And while we've long lifted up
our own system on a pedestal

00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:23.895
as an example for the world,

00:00:23.895 --> 00:00:27.440
we're one of those democracies
that's currently in a yearslong decline.

00:00:28.441 --> 00:00:32.112
So much of the conversation
in the media and among experts

00:00:32.153 --> 00:00:34.281
is about our broken two-party system.

00:00:34.281 --> 00:00:36.366
Democrats vs. Republicans.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.162
And so much of the conversation
about voting is about the outcomes:

00:00:41.204 --> 00:00:43.081
the winners and the losers.

00:00:44.165 --> 00:00:46.209
These are the wrong conversations

00:00:46.251 --> 00:00:49.170
if we want to tackle the threat
to US democracy today.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:54.884
Because we have a much more fundamental
challenge, and it’s quickly growing.

00:00:55.844 --> 00:00:59.222
The United States election
infrastructure is crumbling.

00:01:00.724 --> 00:01:03.852
By infrastructure, I mean the technology,

00:01:03.893 --> 00:01:06.104
the physical infrastructure,
like facilities,

00:01:06.146 --> 00:01:09.024
and most importantly,
the human infrastructure,

00:01:09.065 --> 00:01:12.277
the people who manage
the US voting process.

00:01:12.736 --> 00:01:15.572
Election infrastructure is so essential

00:01:15.614 --> 00:01:17.907
that it's been designated
critical infrastructure

00:01:17.907 --> 00:01:20.368
by the US Department of Homeland Security.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:24.664
That puts it on par with systems
like our power grid and water supply

00:01:24.706 --> 00:01:26.625
in the eyes of the federal government.

00:01:27.083 --> 00:01:29.878
But they haven't invested
in it like it's critical.

00:01:30.837 --> 00:01:34.841
The average portion of a county budget
spent on election operations

00:01:34.841 --> 00:01:37.093
is about half of one percent.

00:01:37.886 --> 00:01:39.929
To put that into context,

00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:43.350
we spend about the same amount
to maintain parking facilities

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.268
as we do our election system.

00:01:46.645 --> 00:01:48.021
If we zoom out a little bit,

00:01:48.063 --> 00:01:52.108
the US election system is actually
pretty unique by global standards.

00:01:52.651 --> 00:01:54.069
Unlike some other places,

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:56.071
we have no central election authority

00:01:56.112 --> 00:01:58.698
that's responsible for managing
the logistics of voting

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.283
for our entire country.

00:02:00.325 --> 00:02:04.162
Instead, we have thousands
and thousands of local departments

00:02:04.204 --> 00:02:06.081
that each have some independent mix

00:02:06.122 --> 00:02:09.042
for figuring out how to make
voting work where they live.

00:02:09.834 --> 00:02:13.129
These departments are staffed
by professional election officials

00:02:13.129 --> 00:02:16.925
with support from volunteer poll workers
during peak election season.

00:02:17.884 --> 00:02:23.139
So with no government-run how-to
for how to administer elections,

00:02:23.181 --> 00:02:27.310
we end up seeing widely different
voting experiences throughout the US,

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:30.522
where voting can legitimately work
one way in one community

00:02:30.522 --> 00:02:33.191
and look totally different somewhere else.

00:02:34.609 --> 00:02:35.777
For over a decade,

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:40.073
my work has focused on providing
technology and training

00:02:40.115 --> 00:02:42.826
and other resources
to state and local election officials

00:02:42.826 --> 00:02:45.203
to support them
in their work serving voters.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:46.788
In my current role,

00:02:46.788 --> 00:02:49.374
that has looked like working
with election departments

00:02:49.374 --> 00:02:50.875
in every corner of the country,

00:02:50.917 --> 00:02:54.629
that together serve about 75 percent
of our eligible voters.

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:57.549
This has given me a really unique window

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:00.802
into what it actually takes
these public servants on the front lines

00:03:00.844 --> 00:03:03.430
to do things like
keep voter rolls up to date

00:03:03.471 --> 00:03:05.932
and quickly and accurately count ballots

00:03:05.974 --> 00:03:08.893
and inform their communities
about how the process works.

00:03:09.936 --> 00:03:12.272
This year-round work is hard.

00:03:12.731 --> 00:03:15.191
And it's often super thankless.

00:03:15.775 --> 00:03:18.236
And the last two years
have been some of the worst.

00:03:19.487 --> 00:03:22.574
Election officials
who serve millions of voters

00:03:22.574 --> 00:03:26.911
currently lack the basic technology
that they need to reliably do their work.

00:03:27.704 --> 00:03:31.291
It either doesn’t exist,
or it’s shockingly outdated.

00:03:31.958 --> 00:03:34.169
In the year 2020,

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:38.840
we supported a small New England town
replace their hand crank ballot box

00:03:38.882 --> 00:03:43.011
that they had been using
since the early 1900s to count ballots.

00:03:43.803 --> 00:03:46.306
One was literally
held together by duct tape.

00:03:48.308 --> 00:03:49.642
Even worse,

00:03:49.642 --> 00:03:53.938
the people that underpin
our voting process,

00:03:53.938 --> 00:03:56.733
the election officials,
are currently under attack.

00:03:57.901 --> 00:04:02.155
In exchange for being outspoken
about the integrity of the process

00:04:02.197 --> 00:04:04.115
that they managed in 2020,

00:04:04.157 --> 00:04:05.325
which, by the way,

00:04:05.325 --> 00:04:10.789
turned out to be our highest turnout
election in the US in over a century

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:14.667
and the most secure election
ever administered in our history,

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:16.920
according to our national
security community --

00:04:16.961 --> 00:04:20.298
(Applause)

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.884
In exchange for the grit and determination

00:04:22.926 --> 00:04:26.346
that it took to make that possible
during a global pandemic,

00:04:26.387 --> 00:04:30.058
today, election officials
are receiving death threats,

00:04:30.058 --> 00:04:32.352
their children are being bullied,

00:04:32.393 --> 00:04:34.896
and some have had to flee their homes.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:39.275
A recent survey shows
that one in three election officials,

00:04:39.317 --> 00:04:40.819
one in three,

00:04:40.819 --> 00:04:43.154
currently feel unsafe doing their work.

00:04:44.197 --> 00:04:45.532
It's appalling.

00:04:45.573 --> 00:04:47.992
And enough is enough.

00:04:49.035 --> 00:04:51.996
We are at a tipping point for US democracy

00:04:52.038 --> 00:04:54.624
and frankly, democracy globally.

00:04:55.041 --> 00:04:56.209
I don't know about you,

00:04:56.209 --> 00:04:58.211
but I personally don't feel comfortable

00:04:58.253 --> 00:05:00.880
just standing around,
leaving things to chance.

00:05:01.339 --> 00:05:06.135
And I especially don't feel comfortable
standing by, asking election officials

00:05:06.177 --> 00:05:09.889
to keep figuring out
how to make it work alone,

00:05:09.931 --> 00:05:12.183
while our system is getting
pushed to its brink.

00:05:13.977 --> 00:05:18.064
We need to rally around a set
of shared values and standards,

00:05:18.106 --> 00:05:19.524
a North Star,

00:05:19.566 --> 00:05:23.361
so that every single voter,
regardless of their zip code,

00:05:23.403 --> 00:05:27.115
has access to a process
that's both fair and trustworthy.

00:05:27.949 --> 00:05:30.785
Election officials need a place
where they can come together

00:05:30.827 --> 00:05:32.495
to keep their skills fresh

00:05:32.537 --> 00:05:35.790
so that they're ready to tackle
whatever challenges might come,

00:05:35.832 --> 00:05:38.626
whether it's training
to bolster cybersecurity

00:05:38.626 --> 00:05:41.087
or to combat disinformation

00:05:41.129 --> 00:05:43.673
or to help them keep voters
safe during a pandemic.

00:05:44.924 --> 00:05:46.885
And every election department,

00:05:46.926 --> 00:05:50.471
rural, urban, large, small,

00:05:50.471 --> 00:05:55.476
needs access to 21st century
secure technology

00:05:55.518 --> 00:05:59.314
so that every single community
has access to the basics.

00:06:00.023 --> 00:06:03.234
Like, a website where voters
can find out answers

00:06:03.234 --> 00:06:05.361
about how voting works where they live.

00:06:05.403 --> 00:06:08.197
Or data-driven tools
that election officials can use

00:06:08.197 --> 00:06:11.367
to make sure that we don't have lines
that snake around city blocks

00:06:11.409 --> 00:06:12.911
outside of polling places.

00:06:15.038 --> 00:06:16.205
Last summer,

00:06:16.247 --> 00:06:20.251
I was at my first conference
since the start of the pandemic,

00:06:20.251 --> 00:06:22.295
and I was going up the escalator

00:06:22.337 --> 00:06:24.589
and I locked eyes
with this election official

00:06:24.631 --> 00:06:26.341
who I absolutely adore.

00:06:26.716 --> 00:06:28.092
Let's call her Sarah.

00:06:29.052 --> 00:06:31.971
I ran and I gave Sarah a huge hug,

00:06:31.971 --> 00:06:36.726
and I realized that probably 60 seconds
passed before we let go.

00:06:37.477 --> 00:06:41.814
And when we did, I was crying
and so was Sarah.

00:06:43.524 --> 00:06:47.737
Sarah's life got flipped totally upside
down after the 2020 election,

00:06:47.737 --> 00:06:51.532
when a vicious conspiracy theory
about her office went viral.

00:06:52.825 --> 00:06:56.746
Almost immediately,
the death threats started to roll in,

00:06:56.788 --> 00:07:01.459
and they were unrelenting
and they were very specific

00:07:01.459 --> 00:07:04.879
about the harm that they wished upon Sarah

00:07:04.921 --> 00:07:06.965
and her husband

00:07:06.965 --> 00:07:08.508
and their children.

00:07:09.175 --> 00:07:13.554
And still, Sarah never backed down
from telling the truth

00:07:13.596 --> 00:07:15.890
about the process that she led.

00:07:17.392 --> 00:07:21.771
In that moment, I was so overwhelmed
with gratitude and love

00:07:21.813 --> 00:07:25.316
and admiration for Sarah
that I couldn't help the tears.

00:07:26.818 --> 00:07:29.070
But I think if I'm being
super, super honest,

00:07:29.112 --> 00:07:34.075
I was just so happy
that my friend was still here to hug.

00:07:36.202 --> 00:07:38.830
Election officials deserve a warm,

00:07:38.871 --> 00:07:43.876
welcoming community where their hard
work is celebrated and encouraged,

00:07:43.876 --> 00:07:45.294
not vilified.

00:07:46.337 --> 00:07:49.298
And all of this is exactly
why I'm so excited

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:53.386
that we are inviting every single
election department in the United States

00:07:53.428 --> 00:07:56.055
to join the US Alliance
for Election Excellence.

00:07:56.097 --> 00:08:00.810
(Applause)

00:08:00.852 --> 00:08:04.188
The Alliance is a place where election
officials and technologists

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:07.191
and designers and other experts
are working hand in hand

00:08:07.233 --> 00:08:09.485
to revitalize US democracy.

00:08:09.527 --> 00:08:13.239
And we're doing it by focusing
on the basics, a shared North Star,

00:08:13.281 --> 00:08:16.117
the tools that it actually takes
to get the job done,

00:08:16.159 --> 00:08:18.703
and a community of support
that has each other's back,

00:08:18.703 --> 00:08:20.329
whatever might come.

00:08:21.748 --> 00:08:27.378
It is absolutely crucial that we rebuild
the foundation of US democracy,

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:30.214
and that's exactly what we're going to do.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:34.677
The Alliance is not only ready
to meet this moment,

00:08:34.677 --> 00:08:37.638
we're ready to create
a foundation so strong,

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:40.475
that we can make it another 200 years.

00:08:40.475 --> 00:08:41.642
Together.

00:08:42.226 --> 00:08:43.394
Thank you.

00:08:43.436 --> 00:08:49.484
(Applause and cheers)

